U2 AND U3 -- the measured output of dev/legendary/U2U3.luau and the asset-path
probe beside it, read off the Studio viewport rather than argued.

THE HEADLINE, BECAUSE IT IS ONE LINE OF SHIPPING CODE:

    MeshPart.Color DOES NOT TINT A TEXTURED MeshPart.

    So SlimeBuilder.build's `body.Color = color` STAYS EXACTLY AS IT IS. The
    per-slime colour simply stops being visible once a skin is bound, which is
    correct by itself -- a painted Legendary is its two authored colours, not
    its palette entry multiplied into them.

==== U3: DOES Color TINT? ====

Three bodies, same sheet, one frame. dev/out/bake/tiger.png went in over HTTP as
raw RGBA and its byte sum was checked against the bake (150405167) BEFORE
binding, so these are the bake's own pixels and not something that merely looks
like them.

    body                     Color        texture
    sink_white               255,255,255  EditableImage (the bake's pixels)
    sink_red                 255,0,0      EditableImage (the bake's pixels)
    control_red_untextured   255,0,0      NONE

Measured off the capture, by clustering each body's pixels to the design's own
two colours:

    body          tangerine px   mean RGB        indigo px   mean RGB
    sink_white          2584   224.4 135.8  52.1      4370   45.7 31.2 74.9
    sink_red            3083   231.0 142.1  54.5      4285   51.3 35.5 84.3
    control_red            0   --                        0   --

    control_red is 7802 px of flat red and NOTHING ELSE.

THE CONTROL IS THE POINT. Without it, "the two textured bodies look the same"
cannot be told apart from "the texture never bound and both fell back to their
Color" -- and those two look identical in the failure case. The control is red,
the other two are not, so the texture is really there. dev/README.md's standing
trap, avoided by construction rather than noticed afterwards.

If Color multiplied, sink_red's tangerine would have G and B near zero. They are
142 and 55, against white's 136 and 52. The hypothesis is not close.

==== U2, PART ONE: THE BINDING ====

Four more bodies, this time with an asset id (the sign board texture already in
the place -- no upload needed to ask this question):

    asset_white_TC   TextureContent = Content.fromUri(id)   bind ok
    asset_red_TC     TextureContent = Content.fromUri(id)   bind ok
    asset_white_TID  TextureID      = id                    bind ok
    asset_red_TID    TextureID      = id                    bind ok

TextureID AND TextureContent ARE THE SAME PROPERTY, which settles a question
that looked like a choice: writing either one reads back on BOTH.

    read back after writing TextureContent:
        TextureID       = "rbxassetid://98429592839825"
        TextureContent  = Content{SourceType=Uri, Uri=rbxassetid://98429592839825}
    read back after writing TextureID: identical.

And Color does not tint on the asset path either. Measured as channel RATIOS,
because absolute brightness varies across the frame:

    body               body px   mean RGB          G/R     B/R
    asset_white_TC        7564   128.0  92.0  55.3  0.719   0.432
    asset_red_TC          7417   136.5  97.5  58.4  0.714   0.428
    asset_white_TID       7411   148.6 105.6  63.3  0.710   0.426
    asset_red_TID         7568   158.9 112.9  68.1  0.711   0.429

THE RATIOS ARE FLAT AND THE BRIGHTNESS IS NOT, and the brightness rises
monotonically LEFT TO RIGHT -- white, red, white, red -- so it tracks POSITION
IN THE FRAME, not Color. That ordering is the control on the residual: if Color
were doing anything, the two red bodies would move together and they do not.

==== U2, PART TWO: THE UPLOAD, AND WHY IT WAS FEARED ====

WHETHER OUR OWN SHEET SURVIVES ROBLOX'S IMAGE ENCODE. Everything above uses
either the bake's raw pixels (no upload) or an image already in the place, so
none of it can answer what an UPLOAD does to a 512x512 sheet whose whole design
is thin high-contrast boundaries -- the narrowest tiger bar is 19.6 px at the
base framing, against an 8 px floor, and lossy recompression is exactly the
thing that eats that margin.

    upload_decal is ADVERTISED by the MCP server and NOT DISPATCHABLE: every
    port answers "Unknown tool: upload_decal" to a tools/call while listing it
    in tools/list. There is no ROBLOSECURITY and no ROBLOX_OPEN_CLOUD_API_KEY
    in the environment, which is what that combination means.

So this needed either a credential on the MCP server or a manual upload.

THE MANUAL UPLOAD HAPPENED. dev/out/bake/tiger.png is rbxassetid://104689541793385.

==== U2, PART TWO: ANSWERED. THE ENCODE IS LOSSLESS. ====

    ROBLOX GAVE BACK EXACTLY THE BYTES THAT WENT UP. Not "within tolerance" --
    identical, alpha included.

Measured in edit mode over the bridge, by running U2_RUNTIME.luau's own
STATS_SRC and MEASURE_SRC blocks rather than a copy of them: the sink serves
the bake's RGBA, CreateEditableImageAsync + ReadPixelsBuffer reads the uploaded
asset back, and the two are held against each other texel by texel.

    asset read back at 512x512 -- no resize
    bake      inMap 262104  markN 117747  edgeN 4230  2A/P 55.6723 tx
    roundtrip inMap 262104  markN 117747  edgeN 4230  2A/P 55.6723 tx  (+0.00%)
    CLASS FLIPS   0 of 262104 in-map texels (0.000%)   off-map flips 0
    TEXEL ERROR   mean 0.00  max 0  over 8: 0.0%  over 32: 0.00%
    full byte sum of the read-back, alpha included: 150405167 == the bake

So the narrowest tiger bar is still 19.6 px at the base framing and 21.1 px at
the reveal, against the 8 px floor, because every texel that defines it came
back unchanged. The upload path costs the design nothing, and the fear that
prompted all of this -- lossy recompression eating a thin high-contrast
boundary -- does not apply to a 512x512 PNG on this account.

THE ZERO WAS CONTROLLED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED. "The asset is byte-for-byte the
bake" and "readAsset is not really reading the asset" produce the same zero, and
they are indistinguishable without a negative control. So the SAME reader was
pointed at the sign board:

    tiger from asset  1048576 bytes  512x512  byte sum 150405167  == the bake
    sign  from asset  1048576 bytes  512x512  byte sum 160324338  differs
    sign  stats: inMap 262144  markN 0  edgeN 0   (bake is 262104 / 117747 / 4230)

A different asset comes back different, and comes back as a plainly different
KIND of image -- every texel in-map, no mark at all. The reader reads what it is
given. dev/README.md's standing trap, closed by construction rather than by
assertion.

WHAT THE FLOOR CHECK WAS WORTH, WHICH IS THE PART TO REMEMBER. featBase came
back at exactly 19.6 px and CLEARS THE FLOOR -- and that line would have read
the same way if the sheet had been mangled. dev/legendary/encode_probe.py put
the bake through six degradations offline BEFORE the run:

    case              featBase  2A/P drift  classflip  meanErr  maxErr
    bake (identity)     19.60px      +0.00%      0.00%     0.00       0
    jpeg q95            19.79px      +0.99%      0.10%     5.18     123
    jpeg q85            19.86px      +1.33%      0.14%     6.65     124
    jpeg q70            19.89px      +1.50%      0.19%     8.59     167
    jpeg q50            19.95px      +1.80%      0.26%    10.65     178
    resize 256->512     20.03px      +2.20%      0.27%     3.56     165
    resize 128->512     20.62px      +5.18%      0.67%     8.99     183

THE 19.6 PX FEATURE NUMBER CANNOT ANSWER THIS QUESTION. It holds within 5% of
19.6 through a 4x downsample that destroys the sheet, and never approaches the
8 px floor in any case. 2A/P is a MEAN characteristic width over the whole mark
set and the wide bars carry it; "is the narrowest bar still above the floor" is
not what it measures. So the probe prints it labelled as a floor check that
cannot fail, and read its verdict off class flips across a = 0.5 and per-texel
error, which move monotonically with damage. Both came back zero, which is what
makes this a result and not a shrug.

That is dev/README.md's standing trap -- a number that stays computable after
the thing it described stopped being the thing you care about -- caught before
the run rather than after it. The pixel-derived 2A/P is not junk: it reproduces
the paint rig's own 19.6 px through both framings' constants, which is what
makes it the right number to PRINT. It is just not the number to READ.

==== ALSO STILL OPEN: THE RUNTIME CONTEXT ====

Everything above was measured in EDIT MODE. Two questions are deliberately not
answered by it, and both are asked by dev/legendary/U2_RUNTIME.luau's installed
probes rather than assumed:

  2a. MAY A SERVER SCRIPT WRITE THE PROPERTY AT ALL? SlimeBuilder's own header
      records RenderFidelity and CollisionFidelity THROWING when written from a
      server or client script -- "the current thread cannot write '<name>'" --
      and they do not fail quietly, they take every slime build down. TextureID
      could be in that family. Edit mode is the permissive context and proves
      nothing about the restrictive one.

  2b. DOES IT CROSS TO A SECOND CLIENT? The server stamps what it wrote as an
      attribute and each client reports what it sees against that stamp, which
      is runtime_mesh_probe's own detector shape pointed at a new property.

2a IS NOW ANSWERED. The probe ran on a real server (twice), and the answer is
that there is no capability problem at all:

    [U2RT server] context: IsServer=true IsClient=false
    [U2RT server] srv_white_TID   via TextureID      write=true  TextureID now "rbxassetid://98429592839825"
    [U2RT server] srv_red_TID     via TextureID      write=true  TextureID now "rbxassetid://98429592839825"
    [U2RT server] srv_white_TC    via TextureContent write=true  TextureID now "rbxassetid://98429592839825"
    [U2RT server] srv_none_red    via nil            write=true  TextureID now ""
    [U2RT server] parented workspace.U2ServerBuilt with 4 bodies

    A SERVER SCRIPT MAY WRITE BOTH PROPERTIES. No throw, no pcall failure, and
    the value reads back on the part. TextureID is NOT in RenderFidelity's
    capability family, which was the thing worth being afraid of.

AND THE ANSWER WAS SITTING IN THE STUDIO LOG THE WHOLE TIME, while every check
available through the MCP bridge said nothing had happened. get_playtest_output
returned outputCount 0; execute_luau answered from a different DataModel and
reported IsRunning=false. The run had gone fine. See dev/README.md -- a silent
channel is not an idle system, and the log file is the channel with no bridge
in it.

THE ROUND TRIP IS NOW CONFIRMED ON A REAL SERVER TOO. The third run was a Play
pressed by hand, and the installed probe repeated the whole measurement in the
play server's own context -- same twenty lines, same zeros, byte sum 150405167.
So "the encode is lossless" is not an edit-mode artefact.

2b IS STILL NOT ANSWERED, AND THE THIRD RUN FAILED FOR A NEW REASON. The first
two were server-only -- no player ever joined. This one HAD a player: the second
Studio process was launched with `-task StartClient -numTestServerPlayersUponStartup 1`
and the server logged `Player -1 added, there are now 1 players total`. The
attach still printed nothing, and the cause was four lines further down the log:

    Error: The current thread cannot write 'Source'
           (lacking capability PluginOrOpenCloud)
    Script 'Workspace.U2ProbeServer', Line 428 - function attach

A RUNTIME SERVER THREAD MAY NOT WRITE Script.Source. The probe's whole strategy
for the client half -- carry the source as a string, build the LocalScript at
runtime, keep it invisible to Rojo by construction -- is not available.

THE IRONY IS WORTH RECORDING. 2a exists because SlimeBuilder saw RenderFidelity
and CollisionFidelity throw "the current thread cannot write '<name>'" from a
server script, and the fear was that TextureID was in that family. It is not --
2a passes. But `Source` IS, and the probe was writing it, in its own installer,
while carefully checking the property that turned out to be safe.

TWO FAILURES THAT LOOK IDENTICAL FROM THE OUTPUT. "No player joined" and "the
attach threw" both present as: server prints everything, client prints nothing.
The first two runs really were the former, which made the third one easy to
misread as more of the same. What separated them was in the log and not in the
probe's own lines -- under CreatorError, not CreatorOutput, so grepping for the
[U2RT] prefix could not see it. GREP THE LOG FOR ERRORS, NOT ONLY FOR YOUR TAG.

THE FIX IS Clone(). The template LocalScript is authored ONCE at install time,
from the command bar, which does hold PluginOrOpenCloud; the server clones it
and reparents it, and a clone copies Source without writing it.

==== 2b: ANSWERED. THE TEXTURE CROSSES. ====

The fourth run, Play pressed by hand with a client. `attached client probe to
Player1` printed, and the client reported on all five bodies:

    body             server wrote                          client sees   verdict
    srv_white_TID    rbxassetid://104689541793385          identical     CROSSED
    srv_red_TID      rbxassetid://104689541793385          identical     CROSSED
    srv_white_TC     rbxassetid://104689541793385          identical     CROSSED
    srv_sign_TID     rbxassetid://98429592839825           identical     CROSSED
    srv_none_red     ""                                    ""            (control)

Colour crossed on every body too. So a server-side TextureID/TextureContent
write REPLICATES, and SlimeBuilder can bind a skin server-side with no client
work at all.

AND THE CLIENT READ THE ASSET BACK ITSELF: inMap 262104, markN 117747, edgeN
4230, 2A/P 55.6723 -- identical to the bake and to the server's own read. The
client is handed the same bytes, which the round trip alone could not say.

ONE WART IN THE OUTPUT, RECORDED SO IT IS NOT READ AS A RESULT: the untextured
control prints "TEXTURE CROSSED" because the comparison is `mine == srvTex` and
both sides are the empty string. That is true but it reads wrong. The control's
job is done by its COLOUR being flat red in the frame, not by that word.

U2 AND U3 ARE NOW CLOSED: 2a writable, 2b replicates, 2c no tint, and the
upload survives byte-for-byte. The probe has been removed from the place.

==== AND THE ANSWER IS WIRED ====

SlimeConfig.SLIME_SKIN_BY_TIER -> SlimeData.Slime.skinTextureId ->
SlimeBuilder.build(color, widthStuds, skinTextureId) -> PlayerProfile's slot
pad, and LaunchRewardScene's reveal so the two agree. Measured on a real server
with a real client, through PlayerProfile.receiveSlime rather than beside it:

    [SKIN server] catalog: #25 Legendary Slime 1 (tier 5 Legendary) skin rbxassetid://104689541793385
    [SKIN server] receiveSlime(Player1, Legendary Slime 1) -> placed on a pad = true
    [SKIN server]   Base_-1.Legendary Slime 1   7.00 studs  Color  70,200, 80  TextureID "rbxassetid://104689541793385"
    [SKIN server]   Base_-1.Uncommon Slime 5    7.00 studs  Color 165, 80,220  TextureID ""
    [SKIN client Player1] skinned body arrived after 1.4 s
    [SKIN client Player1]   Base_-1.Legendary Slime 1   6.82 studs  TextureID "rbxassetid://104689541793385"
    [SKIN client Player1]   Base_-1.Uncommon Slime 5    7.16 studs  TextureID ""

THE UNSKINNED SLIME BESIDE IT IS THE CONTROL and it cost nothing -- the base
already had one. "The tiger has a texture" and "everything has a texture" look
identical without it.

AND THE COLOUR LINE IS THE PROOF THE MEASUREMENT WAS RIGHT. Legendary Slime 1
carries Color 70,200,80 -- GREEN -- and renders tangerine and deep indigo. U3
said Color does not tint a textured MeshPart; that is what it looks like when
the shipping code relies on it. The 6.82 / 7.16 on the client are
SlimeIdleClient's animation around a 7.00 rest, not a scale error.
